The reviewed files cover two distinct signing paths — the field-signing route (`sign-envelope-field.ts`) and the CSC TSP orchestrator (`execute-tsp-sign.ts`) — with meaningfully different status guards on each. [Sources: Reviewed source]

**Source-grounded review**

**Field-signing route: hard gate on `DocumentStatus.PENDING`**

```105:109:packages/trpc/server/envelope-router/sign-envelope-field.ts
    if (envelope.status !== DocumentStatus.PENDING) {
      throw new AppError(AppErrorCode.INVALID_REQUEST, {
        message: `Document ${envelope.id} must be pending for signing`,
      });
    }
```

Any call to `signEnvelopeFieldRoute` on an envelope that is not `PENDING` throws `INVALID_REQUEST` before any write occurs. This is the only envelope-status gate in this file; `COMPLETED`, `REJECTED`, and any other state are all blocked identically.

**Field-signing route: dual recipient-SIGNED guard**

```111:115:packages/trpc/server/envelope-router/sign-envelope-field.ts
    if (recipient.signingStatus === SigningStatus.SIGNED || field.recipient.signingStatus === SigningStatus.SIGNED) {
      throw new AppError(AppErrorCode.INVALID_REQUEST, {
        message: `Recipient ${recipient.id} has already signed`,
      });
    }
```

The guard reads both `recipient` (the token-holder) and `field.recipient` (the field's assigned recipient). For the ASSISTANT role these can be different rows, since the field lookup at lines 42–54 allows an ASSISTANT to match fields belonging to other recipients whose `signingOrder` is eligible.

**CSC path: idempotent return for `SigningStatus.SIGNED`, not a throw**

```110:115:packages/ee/server-only/signing/csc/execute-tsp-sign.ts
  // Idempotency: a 15s tRPC timeout that races with a successful sign can
  // leave the client retrying after the recipient row already flipped to
  // SIGNED. Return success rather than re-running.
  if (recipient.signingStatus === SigningStatus.SIGNED) {
    return { outcome: 'already_signed' };
  }
```

`executeTspSign` returns `{ outcome: 'already_signed' }` rather than throwing when the recipient is already `SIGNED`. The comment explicitly attributes this to the 15 s `Promise.race` in `cscSignEnvelopeRoute` (lines 23–40 of `csc-sign-envelope.ts`). The visible window of `execute-tsp-sign.ts` (lines 8–167) contains no corresponding check for `envelope.status`.

---

**Open questions**

**Does `executeTspSign` enforce `DocumentStatus.PENDING`?**
The field-signing route (Rule 1) throws if the envelope is not `PENDING`, but the reviewed window of `executeTspSign` (lines 8–167) contains no equivalent envelope-status check. The unshown portion of the file may contain one, or the CSC session model may gate it upstream. A test covering "CSC sign attempt when the envelope is `COMPLETED` or `REJECTED`" would either surface the missing guard or confirm the intent is to rely on session validity alone.

**What is the reconciliation policy between the `PROCESSING` computed state and `DocumentStatus.COMPLETED`?**
`signingStatusEnvelopeRoute` returns `PROCESSING` (lines 71–75 of `signing-status-envelope.ts`) whenever the locally-computed `isComplete` predicate is true but the database envelope row has not yet reached `DocumentStatus.COMPLETED`. The reviewed files do not show what drives that final transition or whether there is a timeout. A policy document or test describing the expected latency window between `PROCESSING` and `COMPLETED` — and what happens if the transition never arrives — would clarify whether `PROCESSING` should ever be visible to callers for more than a brief period.

**Is the dual recipient-SIGNED check at line 111 redundant for the ASSISTANT path, or intentional defense-in-depth?**
The field lookup (lines 42–51) already filters ASSISTANT-eligible fields to those whose assigned recipient has `signingStatus: { not: SigningStatus.SIGNED }`, meaning `field.recipient.signingStatus === SigningStatus.SIGNED` should produce a `NOT_FOUND` before line 111 is reached on that branch. Whether the explicit re-check on line 111 is intentional defense-in-depth against a future query change, or an artefact of an earlier code path, is not documented. A comment or test asserting the ASSISTANT-assigned-recipient-already-signed case would confirm the intended layering.

